title: Cisco Optical DOM via CISCO-OPTICAL-MIB
agents: snmp
catalog: hw/network/cisco
license: GPLv2
distribution: community
item: The item is the IOS XR optical pseudo-interface name, for example {Optics0/0/0/0}.
description:
 This check monitors Digital Optical Monitoring information exposed by Cisco
 devices through CISCO-OPTICAL-MIB ({1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.828}). It is intended for
 Cisco IOS XR systems where optical DOM information is provided by this MIB.

 The check monitors RX power and TX power per optical lane in dBm. Laser bias
 values are intentionally not fetched or monitored.

 RX/TX power values and the corresponding low/high thresholds are supplied by
 Cisco in 1/100 dBm and are scaled by the check accordingly.

 The device supplied RX/TX low/high thresholds are always shown in the service
 details. If a threshold is not supplied by the router/transceiver, the detail
 output explicitly states {not provided by device}. These controller thresholds
 are read from CISCO-OPTICAL-MIB OIDs 1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.828.1.1.2.1.7 through
 1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.828.1.1.2.1.10.

 By default the device supplied RX/TX thresholds are used for evaluation. A
 Checkmk rule named {Cisco Optical DOM} can override the thresholds separately
 for RX lower/upper and TX lower/upper levels. Custom Checkmk levels take
 precedence over the values reported by CISCO-OPTICAL-MIB. Device thresholds
 remain visible in the service details even when their use for evaluation is
 disabled.

 The laser status reported by CISCO-OPTICAL-MIB is evaluated only while the
 associated real traffic interface is active. If that traffic interface is
 down, DOM evaluation is skipped because the normal interface check is expected
 to monitor the interface state.
discovery:
 IOS XR exposes DOM data on pseudo interfaces named {Optics0/0/0/x}. Their
 IF-MIB state can remain up even if the corresponding traffic interface is
 down. Therefore this check maps an Optics interface to the real interface with
 the same port path, for example {Optics0/0/0/0} to
 {HundredGigE0/0/0/0} or {FourHundredGigE0/0/0/0}.

 By default a service is discovered only when this associated traffic interface
 has both IF-MIB::ifAdminStatus and IF-MIB::ifOperStatus set to {up(1)}.

 The rule {Cisco Optical DOM discovery} can disable this restriction. When the
 option is disabled, optical controllers containing per-lane data are discovered
 independently of the associated interface state.
